WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.960
Pour cooking oil into water, shake it hard,

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.120
and within a minute the oil floats back

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:06.400
up into its own layer.

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:08.800
Add a few drops of soap, shake it

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.840
again, and the mixture turns cloudy, and stays

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:13.280
cloudy for much longer.

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:16.000
The oil has spread through the water, ready

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:17.160
to rinse away.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:19.520
So how can one substance hold on to

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:21.840
oil and water at the same time?

00:00:23.867 --> 00:00:26.186
First, why won't they mix?

00:00:26.427 --> 00:00:28.746
It's like the hexane layer from the hydrocarbons

00:00:28.827 --> 00:00:29.546
video.

00:00:29.867 --> 00:00:33.626
Oil molecules are mostly long hydrocarbon chains, so

00:00:33.667 --> 00:00:37.066
they're largely non-polar, with mainly dispersion forces to

00:00:37.147 --> 00:00:37.466
offer.

00:00:38.107 --> 00:00:41.066
Letting them in would break water's hydrogen bonds,

00:00:41.147 --> 00:00:43.306
so the water keeps its hydrogen bonds, and

00:00:43.387 --> 00:00:44.826
squeezes the oil out.

00:00:46.834 --> 00:00:49.313
Soap fixes this with a particle that has

00:00:49.434 --> 00:00:51.153
two very different ends.

00:00:51.474 --> 00:00:53.193
Take sodium stearate.

00:00:53.474 --> 00:00:56.033
It's the sodium salt of a long carboxylic

00:00:56.114 --> 00:01:00.513
acid, so its systematic name, sodium octadecanoate, ends

00:01:00.594 --> 00:01:01.633
in oate.

00:01:02.114 --> 00:01:05.473
Stearic acid itself barely mixes with water, like

00:01:05.514 --> 00:01:07.873
the long acids in the carboxylic acids video.

00:01:08.354 --> 00:01:10.513
But in the salt, one end has become

00:01:10.554 --> 00:01:11.713
a charged ion.

00:01:12.354 --> 00:01:14.193
The other end of the stearate ion is

00:01:14.274 --> 00:01:17.273
a chain of seventeen carbons, the tail.

00:01:17.554 --> 00:01:20.593
It's non-polar, so oil attracts it by dispersion

00:01:20.754 --> 00:01:24.833
forces, and it's called hydrophobic, meaning water-avoiding.

00:01:25.474 --> 00:01:28.273
The charged end is the carboxylate group, C

00:01:28.434 --> 00:01:30.193
O O minus, the head.

00:01:30.754 --> 00:01:34.673
Its charge attracts polar water molecules, an ion-dipole

00:01:34.794 --> 00:01:38.353
attraction, so it's hydrophilic, meaning water loving.

00:01:40.367 --> 00:01:42.126
Where does soap come from?

00:01:42.447 --> 00:01:44.766
Fats and oils are triglycerides.

00:01:45.167 --> 00:01:47.966
Each one is glycerol, a small molecule with

00:01:48.047 --> 00:01:50.766
three O H groups, joined to three long

00:01:50.847 --> 00:01:53.406
acid chains by three ester links.

00:01:53.727 --> 00:01:56.926
Heat a fat with concentrated sodium hydroxide, and

00:01:57.007 --> 00:02:00.286
the hydroxide ions break all three ester links.

00:02:00.527 --> 00:02:03.326
You get glycerol, and three soap particles.

00:02:03.807 --> 00:02:06.446
That's saponification, or soap making.

00:02:06.927 --> 00:02:09.646
The sodium hydroxide is used up, three for

00:02:09.727 --> 00:02:12.686
every fat molecule, so it's a reactant, not

00:02:12.767 --> 00:02:13.686
a catalyst.

00:02:13.967 --> 00:02:17.006
The strongly basic mixture also takes each acid's

00:02:17.087 --> 00:02:19.646
proton, leaving a carboxylate ion.

00:02:20.047 --> 00:02:22.846
A carboxylate ion can't re-form the ester with

00:02:22.927 --> 00:02:27.086
glycerol, so unlike esterification, it goes essentially to

00:02:27.167 --> 00:02:27.966
completion.

00:02:29.967 --> 00:02:31.726
Now put soap in water.

00:02:32.207 --> 00:02:34.126
The heads stay in the water, but the

00:02:34.367 --> 00:02:36.846
tails get squeezed out, just like the oil.

00:02:37.407 --> 00:02:39.726
So soap gathers at the surface, with its

00:02:39.807 --> 00:02:41.726
tails sticking up out of the water.

00:02:42.367 --> 00:02:45.086
Add enough soap, and the particles also cluster

00:02:45.167 --> 00:02:47.486
into tiny balls called micelles.

00:02:47.967 --> 00:02:50.046
The tails tuck into the middle, away from

00:02:50.127 --> 00:02:52.446
the water, and the heads face out into

00:02:52.527 --> 00:02:52.766
it.

00:02:54.767 --> 00:02:56.126
Now add grease.

00:02:56.687 --> 00:02:59.246
The tails dissolve into the grease, held there

00:02:59.327 --> 00:03:02.126
by dispersion forces, while the heads stay in

00:03:02.167 --> 00:03:02.686
the water.

00:03:03.327 --> 00:03:06.046
Rubbing or shaking breaks the grease into small

00:03:06.127 --> 00:03:09.406
droplets, each one wrapped in soap, tails in

00:03:09.647 --> 00:03:10.446
and heads out.

00:03:11.167 --> 00:03:14.526
Every droplet's surface is covered in negative heads,

00:03:14.607 --> 00:03:16.766
so the droplets repel each other, and don't

00:03:16.847 --> 00:03:18.206
easily join back together.

00:03:18.847 --> 00:03:20.646
They stay spread through the water as an

00:03:20.687 --> 00:03:24.446
emulsion, tiny droplets of one liquid in another,

00:03:24.527 --> 00:03:26.486
and the rinse carries them away.

00:03:26.927 --> 00:03:29.006
The grease doesn't dissolve in the water.

00:03:29.167 --> 00:03:32.606
It's still oil, just in tiny coated droplets.

00:03:34.634 --> 00:03:38.313
Soap has one big weakness: hard water.

00:03:38.634 --> 00:03:41.753
Hard water has calcium and magnesium ions dissolved

00:03:41.794 --> 00:03:44.473
in it, often from rocks like limestone.

00:03:44.794 --> 00:03:47.913
Two soap anions join one calcium ion, and

00:03:48.034 --> 00:03:50.873
form calcium stearate, which doesn't dissolve.

00:03:51.354 --> 00:03:53.433
It comes out as a sticky white solid

00:03:53.594 --> 00:03:54.673
called scum.

00:03:54.954 --> 00:03:57.513
Two soap ions for each calcium ion keeps

00:03:57.594 --> 00:03:58.793
the charges balanced.

00:03:59.274 --> 00:04:01.593
The soap in the scum can't clean, so

00:04:01.634 --> 00:04:03.993
you get less froth, and need more soap.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:09.040
Synthetic detergents keep the same plan, a long

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:12.000
non-polar tail and a water loving head, but

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:13.120
they change the head.

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:15.760
The most common ones have a sulfonate or

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:19.280
sulfate head, still negative, so calcium ions are

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:20.400
still attracted to them.

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:23.280
But the calcium and magnesium salts they make

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:26.320
are much more soluble, so they stay dissolved,

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:27.840
instead of coming out as scum.

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:30.640
The detergent stays in the water, free to

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:31.480
clean.

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:34.800
Cationic detergents have a positive head, built on

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:37.640
nitrogen, like the ammonium ions from the amines

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:38.520
video.

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:40.840
They're used in fabric softeners.

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:44.120
And non-ionic detergents have no charge at all.

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:46.400
Their head is a polar chain that hydrogen

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:48.960
bonds with water, so calcium ions have nothing

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:49.600
to grab.

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:52.880
So which is better?

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:55.840
Both clean the same way, with micelles.

00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:58.400
A detergent isn't a stronger soap.

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:00.320
It just has a head that makes far

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:01.200
less scum.

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:04.160
Soap is made from fats and oils, a

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:07.520
renewable source, and it breaks down readily, but

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:09.720
it forms scum in hard water.

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:12.880
Detergents work in hard and soft water, but

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:15.680
many are made from petrochemicals, and how fast

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:17.040
they break down varies.

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:19.840
The best choice depends on the water, and

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:20.640
on the job.

00:05:22.667 --> 00:05:24.986
So, head in the water, tail in the

00:05:25.067 --> 00:05:25.706
grease.

00:05:25.947 --> 00:05:28.346
The tails grab the grease, the heads face

00:05:28.427 --> 00:05:30.946
the water, and the charged surfaces keep the

00:05:30.987 --> 00:05:31.946
droplets apart.

00:05:32.507 --> 00:05:35.066
And soap makes scum in hard water, while

00:05:35.107 --> 00:05:36.746
detergents mostly don't.

00:05:38.767 --> 00:05:39.686
Your turn.

00:05:39.967 --> 00:05:42.926
Sodium palmitate is a soap, and its formula

00:05:43.007 --> 00:05:43.886
is shown.

00:05:44.207 --> 00:05:47.486
Identify the hydrophobic and hydrophilic parts of the

00:05:47.567 --> 00:05:48.686
palmitate ion.

00:05:49.247 --> 00:05:52.286
Then explain how this soap removes an oily

00:05:52.367 --> 00:05:55.406
stain from fabric during washing, referring to the

00:05:55.487 --> 00:05:57.606
intermolecular forces involved.

00:05:57.887 --> 00:05:59.166
Pause here to answer.

00:06:10.367 --> 00:06:14.286
The hydrocarbon tail is hydrophobic, and the carboxylate

00:06:14.367 --> 00:06:17.766
head, C O O minus, is hydrophilic.

00:06:18.047 --> 00:06:20.606
The tails dissolve into the oil by dispersion

00:06:20.687 --> 00:06:23.086
forces, and the heads stay in the water

00:06:23.247 --> 00:06:25.206
by ion-dipole attraction.

00:06:25.487 --> 00:06:27.966
Washing lifts the oil off as droplets wrapped

00:06:28.047 --> 00:06:31.246
in soap, with negative surfaces that repel, so

00:06:31.327 --> 00:06:33.566
they stay spread out and rinse away.

00:06:33.887 --> 00:06:36.046
Saying the soap dissolves the oil is the

00:06:36.127 --> 00:06:36.726
trap.

00:06:36.927 --> 00:06:38.206
It's emulsified.

00:06:40.234 --> 00:06:40.873
Last one.

00:06:41.514 --> 00:06:44.313
Soap solution is added to rainwater, and to

00:06:44.394 --> 00:06:47.193
tap water containing dissolved calcium ions.

00:06:47.754 --> 00:06:50.153
The tap water turns cloudy, with a white

00:06:50.314 --> 00:06:51.753
solid and little froth.

00:06:52.474 --> 00:06:54.793
Write a net ionic equation for forming the

00:06:54.874 --> 00:06:57.753
solid, using sodium stearate as the soap.

00:06:58.314 --> 00:07:01.913
Then explain why a sulfonate detergent froths well

00:07:01.954 --> 00:07:03.513
in the same tap water.

00:07:03.834 --> 00:07:05.193
Pause here to answer.

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:19.680
Two stearate ions react with one calcium ion,

00:07:19.880 --> 00:07:22.080
to form solid calcium stearate.

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:25.360
The sodium ions are spectators, so they're left

00:07:25.480 --> 00:07:25.680
out.

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:29.200
The detergent's sulfonate head forms a calcium salt

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:32.400
that's much more soluble, so the detergent stays

00:07:32.440 --> 00:07:34.960
dissolved, and keeps frothing and cleaning.

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:38.160
Writing one stearate with one calcium is the

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:38.800
trap.

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:40.800
The charges don't balance.

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:45.280
So one particle with a water loving head

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:47.760
and a grease loving tail can lift oil

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:48.560
into water.

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:52.080
Next: into the lab, to compare soap and

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:54.560
detergent in soft and hard water.

00:07:56.567 --> 00:07:58.006
Here are the key notes.

00:07:58.247 --> 00:08:00.166
Pause the video if you need more time

00:08:00.247 --> 00:08:01.046
to copy them.
